{"text": "Maya learns most successfully through task analysis with one step at a time; repeated practice in natural settings; model-prompt-practice-feedback sequence; and visual schedule and finished box. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Maya may struggle with abstract language and multi-step directions; generalizing a skill to a new setting; unexpected changes; and reading and mathematics at Grade 8 complexity. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-C-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Maya", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.4625, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.475, "end": 0.8625, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 11}, {"text": "most", "start": 0.875, "end": 1.1375, "start_char": 12, "end_char": 16}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.15, "end": 1.8375, "start_char": 17, "end_char": 29}, {"text": "through", "start": 1.85, "end": 2.075, "start_char": 30, "end_char": 37}, {"text": "task", "start": 2.0875, "end": 2.4375, "start_char": 38, "end_char": 42}, {"text": "analysis", "start": 2.45, "end": 3.0625, "start_char": 43, "end_char": 51}, {"text": "with", "start": 3.075, "end": 3.2125, "start_char": 52, "end_char": 56}, {"text": "one", "start": 3.225, "end": 3.4